WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.933
Modern post-quantum planning should begin with vetted standards and mature implementations, not custom cryptography.

2
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:18.176
NIST's post-quantum standardization work gives organizations a practical reference point for algorithms intended to address major public-key use cases.

3
00:00:18.176 --> 00:00:33.579
This course is not official NIST training, and standards will continue to evolve, but the main engineering message is stable: use reviewed algorithms through well-maintained libraries and protocols rather than inventing new designs.

4
00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:39.586
ML-KEM is the major key-encapsulation concept teams will see in PQC migration discussions.

5
00:00:39.586 --> 00:00:49.829
At a high level, it supports establishing shared secret material in a way intended to resist both classical and cryptographically relevant quantum attacks.

6
00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:53.372
It is not a drop-in answer for every protocol by itself.

7
00:00:53.372 --> 00:01:05.386
It still has to be integrated into TLS, VPNs, APIs, products, certificates where applicable, and operational workflows that expect certain sizes, timings, and negotiation behavior.

8
00:01:05.386 --> 00:01:12.009
ML-DSA and SLH-DSA are digital signature concepts used for different post-quantum signature needs.

9
00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:17.785
ML-DSA is a lattice-based signature family, while SLH-DSA is stateless and hash-based.

10
00:01:17.785 --> 00:01:22.483
Practitioners do not need heavy math to understand the planning impact.

11
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:35.961
They need to know which systems need signatures, who verifies them, how long signatures must remain valid, how large artifacts become, and whether the surrounding infrastructure can handle the new formats.

12
00:01:35.961 --> 00:01:40.351
Algorithms also come with parameter sets and security categories.

13
00:01:40.351 --> 00:01:48.129
Those choices are not decorative; they influence security level, performance, artifact size, and interoperability.

14
00:01:48.129 --> 00:01:54.907
A standards-aware migration still requires testing, monitoring, governance, and rollback planning.

15
00:01:54.907 --> 00:02:07.691
Mature adoption means following reputable implementation guidance, tracking library and vendor support, documenting decisions, and avoiding unsupported combinations that only work in a lab.
